findings.body:
The optional chaining was removed from `openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet`, so `openForm.in_reply_to_snippet` now runs whenever `isOpen` is true.

`MainForm` calls `FormWrapper` without an `openForm` prop. `isOpen` is `editor?.isFocused || hasContent`, so focusing the main comment box or typing in it runs `FormWrapper` with `openForm === undefined`, and that throws a TypeError during render. The whole comments widget goes down on the most common action, not just the reply form.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="311-315"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The main form path is `main-form.tsx:94` (`isOpen`) and `main-form.tsx:98` (`FormWrapper` with no `openForm`). Restoring the `?.` fixes the crash.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="313-313"}
 const isReplyToReply = !!openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet;

The reply payload changed from `openForm.in_reply_to_id` to `parent.id`. For a reply-to-reply, `openForm.in_reply_to_id` is the reply being replied to, but `parent` is the top-level comment, because the reply-to-reply form is rendered inside the top-level `PublishedComment` (`comment.tsx` lines 97-99 and 152).

The backend (`comments-service.js` around line 361) only keeps `in_reply_to_id` when the referenced comment's `parent_id` equals the parent. The top-level comment has `parent_id` null, so the reference is dropped. The reply is then saved with no "Reply to" context, with no error or warning.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="27-37"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The description only asks for the name and expertise fallbacks to be removed. This `in_reply_to_id` change is unrelated to that and is not covered by the new tests.

Revert to `in_reply_to_id: openForm.in_reply_to_id` and keep the dependency array as before. The name and expertise fix does not depend on this line.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="30-30"}
                in_reply_to_id: openForm.in_reply_to_id,

The first new test sets `mockedApi.setMember({name: 'Jane Replier', expertise: null})`. Under the new gate, `setEditable(!!member?.expertise)` evaluates to `false`, so the ProseMirror editor is not contenteditable.

The test then calls `waitEditorFocused(editor)`, which asserts the inner `editor` test id is focused. A non-editable contenteditable element cannot receive focus, so the assertion will likely time out and the test fails. I did not run the suite, so this is based on reading the code.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/test/e2e/actions.test.ts" lines="614-640"}

::callout{type="info"}
The test is discriminating against the base code (base shows the parent's expertise), so it's a good regression test. It needs the editable-gate issue above to be resolved first, or it should drop the focus wait if focus isn't what it is testing.

The second test asserts `expect(memberName).not.toHaveText('Named Author')`. That passes for any text other than the parent's name, including an empty or missing node.

The discriminating part is that the test's comment already says the header should show nothing, 'Anonymous', or a name prompt. `FormHeader` renders 'Anonymous' for a null name, so the test should assert that exact value. That would fail if the header ever rendered blank or the wrong text.

